    "result_text": "All tests pass. Here's what I implemented.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe feature: `POST /api/owners` with `sharesHousehold=true` now assigns the new owner and its existing house-mates (same last name + address) a shared, stable `householdId`, and that id is returned on the owner.\n\nThe pieces, each kept to a single responsibility:\n\n- **`Households` helper** (new, package-private) \u2014 the one place that knows what a \"household\" is: normalizing last-name/address (case-fold + collapse whitespace), finding existing `membersAt(...)`, and deriving the stable `householdId(...)` (SHA-256 of the normalized key, so every member resolves to the same value without a lookup).\n- **`EnsureUniqueHousehold`** \u2014 refactored to reuse `Households.membersAt`, so the guard and the assignment share matching logic instead of duplicating it. Behaviour unchanged: reject a collision unless `sharesHousehold` is set.\n- **`AssignHousehold`** (new function) \u2014 runs after `BuildOwner`, before `SaveOwner`, inside the transaction. When opted in and house-mates exist, it stamps the shared id on the built owner and back-fills any existing member lacking one (so \"both get the same id\"). No opt-in / no house-mates \u2192 leaves it unset.\n- **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 new `assignHousehold` step wired between `assignCustomerCode` and `save`.\n- **`Owner` entity** \u2014 new `householdId` field / `household_id` column.\n- **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 `householdId` added to the `Owner` schema as read-only, so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it and MapStruct maps it automatically (returned on both create and GET).\n- **All four `schema.sql`** (hsqldb/h2/postgres/mysql) \u2014 added the `household_id` column (tests run on hsqldb with `ddl-auto=none`, so the column had to be in the schema).\n\n## Verification\n\n- `AcceptanceTest.coreJoinersShareHouseholdId` \u2014 passes (B and C get equal, non-blank `householdId`).\n- Full `./mvnw test` \u2014 green (exit 0, 0 failures/errors).\n\nOne design note worth flagging: `householdId` is derived deterministically from the normalized last-name+address, and existing members are back-filled when a new member joins \u2014 so the id is stable and identical across the whole household regardless of creation order. The `PUT` edit path was intentionally left untouched (it doesn't manage `customerCode`/`registrationDate`/household either), consistent with the existing code.",
